import type { ResearchPost } from './fleet-data';

export const novemberNineteenResearchBatch: readonly ResearchPost[] = [{"slug":"developeroffshore-alert-fatigue-as-a-support-cost-in-distributed-offshore-operations","title":"Alert fatigue as a support cost in distributed offshore operations","excerpt":"A research review of alert fatigue in offshore support: how to define it, where the cost appears, how routing and ownership shape it, what to measure, and a practical review protocol.","published":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":["This report examines alert fatigue as an operating cost in distributed software support. The unit is one paging alert delivered to a support rotation, together with the action it triggers and the time it consumes. The scope covers production alerts handled by an offshore pod, including the overlap hours shared with the buyer. It excludes marketing notifications, build failures, and routine ticket updates that never page anyone.","The question is whether a buyer can see the cost of alert volume without treating every page as equally valuable. A busy rotation can look productive while a few useful alerts are buried under noise. The report treats fatigue as a reliability problem for the human signal path, not as a complaint about effort. It looks for evidence that an alert earns its interruption."]},{"heading":"What counts as alert fatigue","body":["Fatigue appears when the response to an alert becomes weaker because the alert has fired before without consequence. The visible sign is a slow acknowledgement or a quick dismissal. The underlying condition is a mismatch between the number of interruptions and the number of decisions they require. A single noisy alert can create that mismatch for an entire rotation.  That mismatch is a design problem in the alert set, not a personal failing of the responder.","The definition should include the context in which the alert arrives. An alert at 02:00 local time costs more than the same alert during a staffed overlap hour, even when the technical severity is identical. Record the local time and the responder's shift when measuring the burden. A team that ignores the clock will undercount the real disruption.  Two alerts with the same severity can carry very different costs depending on when they arrive."]},{"heading":"Where the cost appears","body":["The first cost is attention. A responder who checks a channel every few minutes cannot stay inside a difficult investigation. The second cost is trust, because a channel that mostly produces false alarms stops being a signal. The third cost is turnover, since engineers on a noisy rotation often leave or ask to be removed.  Each of those costs shows up in a different measure, so a single count will miss at least one of them.","The cost also appears in the handoff. A page that arrives near the end of a shift can be acknowledged by one engineer and investigated by another, losing the context that made it understandable. When alerts are frequent, the handoff note becomes a queue of half-finished stories. The buyer pays for that lost context through slower resolution and repeated questions."]},{"heading":"Signal design and routing","body":["An alert should name the user-visible condition, the service, and the first check to run. A title such as high error rate on checkout tells the responder where to look. A title that says monitor triggered forces a search before the investigation can begin. The alert body should include the dashboard and the runbook link rather than a wall of raw metrics.","Routing matters as much as wording. An alert sent to a group channel with no assigned responder is a notification, not a page. Use a rotation with a primary and a backup, and send paging alerts through a channel that requires acknowledgement. Informational alerts belong in a separate stream that the team can read when it has capacity.  The routing rule should also say what happens when the primary does not acknowledge within the agreed time.","A useful rule is to page only when a person must act before the next business window. Conditions that can wait should create a ticket with an owner and a due date. This distinction keeps the paging channel small enough to trust and gives the non-urgent work a path that does not depend on memory.  The rule should be written down so a new engineer can apply it without asking for an exception."]},{"heading":"Measuring the load","body":["Count pages per shift, acknowledgement time, and the share of pages that led to a real action. Those three numbers describe volume, responsiveness, and value. A rotation with many pages and a low action share is a candidate for redesign, even if the acknowledgement time looks healthy.  Report the three numbers together so a low action share cannot hide behind a fast acknowledgement.","Segment the counts by alert rule, service, and local hour. A single rule can dominate the total and hide the rest of the picture. The local-hour view shows whether the burden falls on one shift or is spread across the team. Review the segments with the responders before proposing a target.  A rule that dominates one shift is a different problem from a rule that is spread across the whole month.","Pair the counts with the incidents that had no alert. A quiet rotation is not necessarily a healthy one, because a missing signal can be worse than a noisy one. Sample recent incidents and ask whether an existing alert should have fired. That check keeps the review from rewarding silence.  The sample should include incidents from different services rather than only the ones that already have alerts."]},{"heading":"Ownership and review","body":["Every paging rule needs an owning team and a named maintainer. The owner decides the threshold, the severity, and the response expectation. Without an owner, a rule survives long after the condition it described has changed. The owner should also be able to retire the rule when the service no longer needs it.  The owner should also know which downstream teams depend on the service, because a threshold change can affect them.","Review the paging set monthly with the support lead and the buyer's service owner. Bring the top rules by volume and the rules that produced no action. Pick one or two to rewrite or remove, then measure the same signals for a month. A review that changes nothing is a meeting, not a control.  Record the decision and the date so the next review can tell whether the change worked."]},{"heading":"A practical protocol","body":["Export one month of paging history with the rule, the service, the local time, the acknowledgement, and the action taken. Classify each page as action, investigation only, duplicate, or noise. Use a short classification guide so two reviewers apply the same rule. Check a sample together before trusting the totals.  Keep the export in a shared location so the reviewer can repeat the classification without asking for the raw file.","Rank the rules by total interruptions and by the share classified as noise. Pick the top rule and inspect its threshold, its routing, and its runbook. Change one variable, such as the threshold or the severity, and record the change date. Compare the next month with the same classification method.  If the top rule is a symptom alert, check whether the underlying cause has its own signal.","Share the result with the responders before the buyer. The people who received the pages know which ones were useful and which ones arrived at a bad time. Their account often explains a pattern that the ticket data cannot show, such as a deploy that always triggers the same warning.  Their account may also reveal a page that arrived during a handoff and was never properly investigated."]},{"heading":"A worked example","body":["A support pod receives about ninety pages in a month across four services. The review finds that one disk-space rule accounts for thirty-five pages, and none of them led to an action because the cleanup job always ran before the threshold mattered. The rule was written for a smaller volume and never revisited after the storage upgrade.  The team can use that history to show why the threshold changed, which makes the next review easier.","The team lowers the rule to a ticket and adds a capacity review to the monthly maintenance list. The next month the paging total falls by roughly a third. The remaining pages are easier to investigate because the channel is no longer dominated by a known non-event. No new tooling was required for the change.  The comparison should use the same four-week window and the same classification guide as the baseline."]},{"heading":"Data to collect first","body":["Record the alert rule, the service, the severity, the local time, the responder, the acknowledgement time, and the action taken. Add the incident or ticket that followed and whether the alert was the first signal. Keep the raw export so a reviewer can recheck the classification without rebuilding the report.  The record should also note whether the page arrived during a planned release or an incident.","Collect at least four weeks before drawing a conclusion. One busy week can distort the picture, especially around a release or a holiday. If the paging tool changes, mark the change and start a new comparison period. A mix of two tools in one trend is hard to interpret.  Mark the period and the tool version beside the counts so a later reader knows what changed."]},{"heading":"Counterevidence and limits","body":["A high page count can be appropriate during a genuine incident or a major migration. The review should separate planned bursts from steady noise. Removing alerts during a period of real risk can reduce the team's awareness just when it is needed. Label exceptional periods rather than averaging them into the baseline.  A planned burst should be reviewed with the release owner, not treated as evidence of a bad alert set.","Alert value is also a judgment, not a pure count. A rare page that catches a data-loss condition is worth more than ten routine pages, even if the routine ones are cheaper to handle. Use the count to start the conversation and the incident sample to check whether the signal is still doing its job.  The judgment should be recorded so the next reviewer can see why a rare alert was kept."]}],"sources":[{"name":"Google SRE Workbook, Alerting on SLOs","url":"https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/"},{"name":"PagerDuty Incident Response, Alerting Principles","url":"https://response.pagerduty.com/oncall/alerting_principles/"},{"name":"NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 2, Computer Security Incident Handling Guide","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r2/final"}],"keyStats":["4 page classes: action, investigation only, duplicate, and noise","3 load measures: pages per shift, acknowledgement time, and action share","2 views: by alert rule and by local hour","1 monthly decision: rewrite, downgrade, or retire the noisiest rule"],"takeaways":["Define fatigue by the response it produces, not by the number of alerts alone.","Measure the local hour as part of the burden, because an overnight page costs more than an overlap-hour page.","Page only when someone must act before the next business window, and route the rest to owned tickets.","Review the top rules by volume and action share every month with the responders present.","Sample incidents with no alert so a quiet channel does not get mistaken for a healthy one."],"faqs":[{"question":"Is a lower page count always better?","answer":"No. A missing alert can be worse than a noisy one. Pair the count with an incident sample that asks whether an existing signal should have fired."},{"question":"Who should own an alert rule?","answer":"The team that responds to it should maintain it, with a named person and a buyer-side counterpart for cross-service rules. Ownership includes the authority to retire it."},{"question":"How often should the paging set be reviewed?","answer":"Monthly is enough for volume and action share, with a deeper review after a major architecture change. Quarterly is too slow for a rule that fires every night."}],"related":[{"title":"On-call load distribution in distributed offshore support","href":"/research/developeroffshore-on-call-load-distribution-in-distributed-offshore-support"},{"title":"Set an alert ownership rule for an offshore support pod","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-set-an-alert-ownership-rule-for-an-offshore-support-pod"},{"title":"Incident recurrence rate in outsourced application support","href":"/research/developeroffshore-incident-recurrence-rate-in-outsourced-application-support"}]},{"slug":"developeroffshore-query-performance-regression-as-a-maintenance-signal-in-outsourced-work","title":"Query performance regression as a maintenance signal in outsourced work","excerpt":"A research review of query performance regression in outsourced maintenance: how to define it, build a baseline, read plans and statistics, separate workload drift, and run a review.","published":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":["This report examines query performance regression as a maintenance signal in outsourced database work. The unit is one query or query family whose response time or resource use becomes materially worse after a change. The scope covers application queries in relational databases, including reporting statements and background jobs. It excludes capacity incidents caused by hardware failure or a network outage.","The question is whether a pod can detect and explain a regression before users report it. A query that slows by twenty percent may be invisible in a dashboard that only tracks averages. The report treats regression as a change in the relationship between a query and its data, plan, or call pattern. It looks for evidence that the change is real and attributable."]},{"heading":"Defining a regression","body":["A regression needs a comparison point. Define the baseline query, the data window, and the statistic that will be compared, such as median latency, rows scanned, or shared blocks read. Without a fixed comparison, a slow day looks like a regression and a fast cache looks like an improvement. Write the definition before the review starts.  Put the definition in the review template so every comparison uses the same fields.","Set a materiality threshold that fits the user experience. A jump from twenty to thirty milliseconds may not matter, while a jump from two to four seconds will. The threshold should be tied to a page budget or a job window rather than a round percentage. Record the threshold beside the measurement so a later reader can judge the decision."]},{"heading":"Baseline and comparison window","body":["Capture the baseline during a period that represents normal use, not during a release freeze or a holiday. Record the query text, the plan, the row counts, and the database version at that time. A baseline without a plan cannot distinguish a code change from a statistics change.  A baseline taken after a release can include the release's own effect and make the comparison misleading.","Compare the same business window, because query load follows the calendar. A report that runs at month end has a different profile from the same report on a Tuesday morning. If the comparison must span different periods, say so and adjust the interpretation rather than the number.  Record the window in the review so a later reader can tell whether the comparison was fair."]},{"heading":"Plan and statistics evidence","body":["Read the plan for both periods and compare the access paths, join order, and row estimates. A change from an index scan to a sequential scan is a concrete finding. So is a join order that reverses because the planner now expects far fewer rows from one side. Record the estimated and actual rows for the steps that changed.  The plan comparison should use the same query text, because a rewritten statement is a different unit.","Stale statistics are a common cause of a plan shift after a bulk load or a migration. Check the last analyze time and the sample size before assuming the query itself is at fault. Refresh the statistics in a controlled way and compare again. A plan that returns to the old shape after an analyze points to maintenance, not to code."]},{"heading":"Workload and data drift","body":["The same query can slow because the table grew, because a tenant added a large data set, or because a caller started running it more often. Separate execution time from call volume. A query that is stable at five milliseconds but now runs a thousand times per request can still double the page time.  The call count is often the missing number in a query review, and it can change without a code edit.","Check whether the data distribution changed. A predicate that used to match a few rows can begin matching a large fraction after a product change. The planner may be making a reasonable choice for the new distribution. Look at the row counts before concluding that the plan is wrong.  A distribution change is a reason to revisit the index, not necessarily a reason to rewrite the query."]},{"heading":"Review ownership","body":["The pod can gather the evidence and propose a fix, while the buyer-side data owner approves changes that affect shared tables or indexes. Assign the review to one named engineer and one reviewer so the comparison has an owner. A committee can discuss a regression for weeks without producing a decision.  The owner should be able to approve a test in a read replica without waiting for a full change window.","Keep the query review separate from a general performance project. One query family, one hypothesis, and one measured result is enough for a review cycle. A broad performance initiative tends to produce a long list of possible causes and no change to the signal.  A narrow review also makes the result easier to explain to the buyer's data owner. The result should be attached to the change record so the next reviewer can read it."]},{"heading":"A practical protocol","body":["Select the query from a user complaint, a slow-query log, or a changed endpoint. Record the current plan, timing, and call count. Find the last known good period and capture the same fields. If no baseline exists, capture the current state and mark the first review as establishing one.  Write down the chosen period before running the comparison so the result cannot be selected after the fact.","Form one hypothesis about the cause: plan change, statistics, data growth, or call volume. Test it with the smallest safe change, such as an analyze or a query rewrite in a read replica. Compare the same statistic over the same window. Record the result whether or not it confirms the hypothesis.  A negative result is useful because it removes a hypothesis and narrows the next test.","Add a monitor that compares the query with its baseline and alerts only when the threshold is crossed. Give the monitor an owner and a review date. Without a follow-up, the same regression can return after the next migration or bulk load.  The monitor should use the same statistic and window as the review rather than a different dashboard default."]},{"heading":"A worked example","body":["A monthly statement query takes four seconds after a migration and took one second before it. The plan shows a sequential scan on a table that grew from two million to nine million rows. The index exists, but the predicate applies a function to the indexed column, so the planner cannot use it.  The row growth alone does not prove the cause, but it explains why the plan changed.","The team rewrites the predicate to compare the raw column and adds a test that checks the plan shape. The query returns to about one second in the test environment and stays within the page budget after release. The review records the function-on-column cause so the next engineer recognizes the pattern.  The test should fail when the predicate wraps the column again, so the fix survives a later refactor."]},{"heading":"Data to collect first","body":["Record the query text, the caller, the baseline window, the comparison window, the median and tail latency, the rows examined, and the plan. Add the database version, the statistics timestamp, and the data volume at each point. Keep the raw plan output so a reviewer can inspect the steps that changed.  The record should note whether the plan came from a test system or production, because the plans can differ.","Collect enough runs to see a range rather than a single sample. Five runs on an idle machine are weaker than a week of production samples. If the query is rare, compare the same scheduled run across several periods. Mark the sample size beside the result.  A range with a wide spread is a signal to inspect the environment before changing the query."]},{"heading":"Counterevidence and limits","body":["A slower query is not always a problem worth fixing. A report that runs once a month and finishes inside its window may be fine even after a large relative increase. Weigh the regression against the user impact and the cost of the change. A fix that adds a risky index to a high-write table can be worse than the delay.","Plans also vary for reasons the team cannot control, including concurrent load and cache state. A single comparison can mislead. Repeat the measurement and look for a consistent pattern before committing to a database change. The review should state which explanation the evidence supports and which one remains open. A second measurement after a week is enough to confirm the pattern."]}],"sources":[{"name":"PostgreSQL Documentation, Using EXPLAIN","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html"},{"name":"PostgreSQL Documentation, pg_stat_statements","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/pgstatstatements.html"},{"name":"MySQL Documentation, The Slow Query Log","url":"https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/slow-query-log.html"}],"keyStats":["4 likely causes: plan change, stale statistics, data growth, and call volume","3 statistics to compare: median latency, rows examined, and shared blocks read","2 fixed points: the same business window and the same data shape","1 rule: establish a baseline before calling a change a regression"],"takeaways":["Define the baseline window, the query, and the statistic before reviewing a slowdown.","Compare plans and row estimates together so a code change is not confused with a statistics change.","Separate execution time from call volume, because a stable query can still slow a page.","Test one hypothesis at a time and keep the result even when it is negative.","Weigh the fix against write cost and user impact instead of optimizing every slower query."],"faqs":[{"question":"Should every slower query be treated as a regression?","answer":"No. A report with a generous window can absorb a relative slowdown. Use a materiality threshold tied to user impact or a job deadline."},{"question":"Who should approve a new index?","answer":"The buyer-side data owner should approve indexes on shared or high-write tables. The pod can prepare the plan comparison and the write-cost estimate."},{"question":"What if there is no historical baseline?","answer":"Capture the current state, mark it as the first baseline, and compare future runs against it. State the limitation in the review so the first result is not overread."}],"related":[{"title":"Set a query performance review for an offshore database task","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-set-a-query-performance-review-for-an-offshore-database-task"},{"title":"Test data refresh risk in offshore development","href":"/research/developeroffshore-test-data-refresh-risk-in-offshore-development"},{"title":"Environment drift cost in long-running offshore engagements","href":"/research/developeroffshore-environment-drift-cost-in-long-running-offshore-engagements"}]},{"slug":"developeroffshore-change-advisory-lead-time-in-offshore-maintenance-windows","title":"Change advisory lead time in offshore maintenance windows","excerpt":"A research review of change advisory lead time in offshore maintenance: the start and end of the clock, the four segments, risk tiers, time-zone coordination, and a review protocol.","published":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":["This report examines change advisory lead time for maintenance windows staffed by an offshore pod. The unit is one proposed change from the moment it is ready for review to the moment a named approver authorizes the window. The scope covers the advisory steps that sit between a finished change plan and an approved maintenance slot. It excludes the build work and the execution of the change itself.","The question is whether the advisory path adds avoidable delay or appropriate control. A short lead time can mean a well-prepared team, or it can mean nobody reviewed the risk. The report decomposes the path so a buyer can see where a change waits and which wait is worth keeping. It treats lead time as a coordination measure, not a speed target."]},{"heading":"Start and end of the clock","body":["Choose a start the delivery team controls, such as the moment the change note and rollback evidence are complete. A start at the customer request date mixes discovery and approval, which makes the result hard to act on. The start should mean the pod has done its part and is waiting on a decision.  Write the start rule in the change template so a submitter cannot choose a later date by accident.","Choose an end that reflects real authority, such as the approval recorded in the change system. A verbal yes in a meeting is not the end unless it is written down. If the approval only becomes usable when the window is scheduled, record both times and report them together.  The end rule should also say who records the approval when the approver is unavailable."]},{"heading":"The four segments","body":["Split the advisory path into preparation, review assignment, reviewer decision, and scheduling. Preparation is the time to complete the note and evidence. Review assignment is the wait for a qualified reviewer. Reviewer decision is the time that reviewer spends before approving or returning the change. Scheduling is the wait for a window that fits the risk, the approver, and the calendar.","Report each segment as a share of the total. A ten-day path with one day of review and seven days of scheduling points to a different fix than a path with six days of reviewer silence. The share shows where the coordination cost sits, while the absolute number shows how much room there is to change it.  The share is more useful than the total when the team compares changes of different risk.","Check that the segments add up to the total. A gap usually means a step nobody named, such as a security sign-off that happens outside the change tool. Reconcile the gap before using the number for a decision. An incomplete decomposition can send the team toward the wrong bottleneck.  A missing segment is a finding in itself because it means the process has an unmeasured step."]},{"heading":"Risk tiers and proportionality","body":["A routine configuration change and a schema migration should not follow the same approval path. Use a small number of risk tiers and define what each tier requires. The tier should raise the evidence and the approver level, not simply add waiting. A tier that only adds a meeting teaches the team to avoid it.  The tier definitions should name the evidence required, not only the title of the approver.","Review the tier assignment when a change is returned or when an incident follows an approval. A change that needed more review than its tier allowed is evidence that the criteria are too loose. A change that waited for an approver who added no value is evidence that the tier is too broad.  Review the tier definitions at the same time as the approval data so the labels stay meaningful."]},{"heading":"Coordination across time zones","body":["Every handoff between the pod and the buyer adds a waiting period that is bounded by the overlap window. A reviewer who is available for two hours per day will approve fewer changes than one who shares a longer overlap, even when both work at the same speed. Record the overlap and the reviewer's queue depth beside the lead time.  The overlap should be recorded in the reviewer roster so the pod can plan around it.","Batch approvals can reduce the number of handoffs, but they also increase the time each change waits for the next batch. A daily review slot may be better than an ad hoc request that depends on catching someone online. Test the schedule with the actual reviewers before changing the process.  The test should include a change submitted after the cut-off to see how the schedule handles it."]},{"heading":"Measurement pitfalls","body":["An average hides the changes that wait for weeks because a single approver is on leave. Report the median and the share above a threshold, such as three business days. The tail is where the coordination failure usually lives. Segment by risk tier so a rare migration does not distort the routine path.  The tail view should name the changes that waited longest so the team can inspect the cause.","A change can look fast if the clock starts late. Moving the start to the review request excludes the preparation wait, which may be the largest part. Keep the definition stable and publish it beside the chart. A trend built on a changed definition is not comparable with the old one.  Publish the definition beside the trend so a reader can compare periods without guessing."]},{"heading":"A practical protocol","body":["Instrument four timestamps: ready for review, reviewer assigned, decision recorded, and window scheduled. Add the risk tier, the reviewer, and the overlap window. Collect one quarter before comparing teams or periods. Check a sample of changes against the tool history to confirm the timestamps are real.  The sample should include changes that were returned, because a return adds a segment the timestamps may not capture.","Pick the largest segment and change one thing. If assignment is the bottleneck, publish a reviewer roster for each tier. If scheduling is the bottleneck, reserve a weekly window for routine changes. Measure the same segments for a month and record the result with the change date.  Record the change date beside the result so the next review can separate the fix from other events.","Review the result with the approvers as well as the pod. The approvers can explain a delay that the data only hints at, such as a missing security review or a change that was not ready. Their account should lead to a process fix rather than a request to respond faster.  The conversation should produce one process change with an owner and a review date."]},{"heading":"A worked example","body":["A pod submits twelve maintenance changes in a quarter. The median advisory lead time is eight business days. The decomposition shows one day of review and six days of scheduling because the only approved window is a Saturday night that the buyer's database administrator must staff.  The Saturday constraint is a capacity fact, not a failure of the pod, and the review should say so.","The team adds a Tuesday window for low-risk changes and keeps the Saturday slot for migrations. The median falls to three days, while the review segment stays the same. The change adds capacity for routine work without weakening the approval step that matters for risky changes.  The result should be compared over the same quarter length before the team treats it as stable."]},{"heading":"Data to collect first","body":["Record the change identifier, the risk tier, the ready time, the assigned time, the decision time, the scheduled window, and the approver. Add whether the change was returned and the reason. Keep the raw timestamps so a reviewer can recheck the segments rather than trusting a summary.  The record should also note whether the change was returned to the submitter and how long that took.","Collect at least a quarter, because maintenance windows are often monthly and a single cycle is too small to show a pattern. Mark periods when the approval policy changed. If the tool does not record assignment, use the first reviewer comment as a proxy and state the limitation.  A quarter with a policy change should be split rather than averaged into one number."]},{"heading":"Counterevidence and limits","body":["A long advisory lead time can be appropriate for a high-risk change that needs a security review and a rollback rehearsal. The measure should not push a team to skip evidence. Compare the path with the risk tier and the incident history before calling the delay waste.  The review should separate a deliberate delay from an unowned queue before proposing a fix.","Lead time also depends on the number of qualified approvers, which may be outside the pod's control. A buyer with one database administrator will have a different path from one with a team. Report the constraint honestly and treat the result as a prompt to inspect the process, not as a score for the pod.  The result should be reported with that constraint so the reader does not read it as a pod score."]}],"sources":[{"name":"DORA, DORA Metrics and the Four Keys","url":"https://dora.dev/guides/dora-metrics-four-keys/"},{"name":"NIST SP 800-128, Guide for Security-Focused Configuration Management","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/128/final"},{"name":"Google SRE Workbook, Canarying Releases","url":"https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/"}],"keyStats":["4 advisory segments: preparation, assignment, decision, and scheduling","3 timestamps to trust: ready, decision recorded, and window scheduled","2 views: median lead time and the share above the tier threshold","1 rule: report the median and the tail, never the average alone"],"takeaways":["Start the clock when the change note and evidence are complete, not when the idea first appears.","Separate preparation, assignment, decision, and scheduling so the largest wait is visible.","Match the approval path to a small number of risk tiers instead of adding meetings to every change.","Include the reviewer's overlap window and queue depth when explaining a delay.","Report the median and the share above a threshold, because one parked change can distort an average."],"faqs":[{"question":"Should advisory lead time include weekends?","answer":"Use business time for the decision segments and calendar time for the scheduled window. State which clock each segment uses so the total is interpretable."},{"question":"Is a shorter advisory path always better?","answer":"No. A high-risk change may need a security review and a rehearsal. Judge the path against the risk tier and the incident history."},{"question":"What if the buyer has only one approver?","answer":"Record the constraint and a backup plan for leave. A single approver is a capacity limit, not a reason to remove the approval."}],"related":[{"title":"Write a change advisory note for an offshore maintenance window","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-write-a-change-advisory-note-for-an-offshore-maintenance-window"},{"title":"Change failure rate as a contract health signal in offshore delivery","href":"/research/developeroffshore-change-failure-rate-as-a-contract-health-signal-in-offshore-delivery"},{"title":"Queue wait time as a delivery constraint in offshore code review","href":"/research/developeroffshore-queue-wait-time-as-a-delivery-constraint-in-offshore-code-review"}]},{"slug":"developeroffshore-production-data-access-breadth-in-offshore-support-teams","title":"Production data access breadth in offshore support teams","excerpt":"A research review of production data access breadth in offshore support: what breadth means, how standing access grows, task-scoped grants, evidence, measurement, and a review protocol.","published":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":["This report examines the breadth of production data access held by an offshore support team. The unit is one account or role and the set of production data it can read, export, or change. The scope covers support engineers and maintenance developers who work under a buyer's data policy. It excludes service accounts used by the application itself.  The boundary matters because a support engineer and an application account carry different review expectations.","The question is whether a buyer can see how much production data the support pod can reach and whether that reach matches the work. A broad grant is not automatically a failure, because support sometimes needs to inspect a record to resolve a case. The report treats breadth as a design choice that should be visible, reviewed, and reversible.  The report also treats a narrow grant with a long duration as a breadth problem, because time multiplies exposure."]},{"heading":"What breadth means","body":["Breadth has two dimensions: the number of people with access and the amount of data each person can reach. A small team with a warehouse-wide read role can have more breadth than a larger team limited to a support console. Measure both dimensions rather than counting accounts alone.  A count of accounts can look small while one role reaches every tenant in the warehouse.","Include the ability to export, not only the ability to view. An export turns a narrow view into a portable copy that can leave the controlled system. The same record seen on a screen and downloaded as a file carry different risks. Record which roles permit export and where the files can go.  Export should be reviewed separately because a file can be copied after the session ends."]},{"heading":"Why standing access grows","body":["Standing access accumulates through small exceptions. An engineer needs one table for a case, and the simplest grant is a role that covers the whole schema. The grant stays after the case closes because nobody owns the removal. A year later the role is part of onboarding and no one remembers why.  The renewal step should ask for the original purpose, not only for a signature.","Shared accounts and copied credentials also widen access without a visible grant. A team that shares an administrator login can reach anything that login can reach, regardless of the role assigned to each engineer. The audit sees one account and misses the number of people behind it. Named accounts are a prerequisite for measuring breadth at all.  Replacing shared credentials is the first control because it makes every later measurement meaningful."]},{"heading":"Task-scoped access","body":["Task-scoped access ties a grant to a case, a data set, and an expiry. The engineer requests the view needed for the task, and the approver sees the purpose and the duration. The grant can be narrow because the task is narrow. This model costs more approvals, so it works best for sensitive data and exceptional requests.  A task-scoped grant should also name the reviewer who can extend it if the case runs long.","A support console can provide a middle path. It offers a limited set of queries or masked views without giving direct database credentials. The console logs each lookup and can enforce a purpose field. The team gets the record it needs, and the buyer keeps a central record of who looked at what.  The console should record the purpose field even when the lookup returns no rows."]},{"heading":"Evidence and review","body":["Each grant should record the requester, the approver, the data scope, the purpose, and the expiry. The record belongs in the same system that grants the access so the audit can follow the chain. A spreadsheet maintained beside the identity provider tends to drift from the real permissions.  The grant record should be queryable so the next review does not depend on a manual spreadsheet.","Review the grant against actual use. A role that has not been used in a quarter may still be needed for an emergency, but that is a decision to record rather than assume. Look at the audit log for the queries and exports the role enabled. Usage evidence turns the review from a permission list into a risk conversation.  A role with no use and no emergency note is a candidate for removal, not an automatic keep."]},{"heading":"Measuring breadth","body":["Build a matrix of roles and data domains, then mark which roles can read, export, or write each domain. Count the people assigned to each role. The matrix shows where a single role spans unrelated domains, which is often the clearest sign of over-breadth.  The matrix should be reviewed with the support lead because the labels on paper may not match the work.","Track exceptions separately from standard roles. Exceptions are the grants most likely to be forgotten and the ones a reviewer should inspect first. Report the number of open exceptions, their age, and the domains they touch. A stable exception count with old entries is a sign that the removal step is not working.  An exception with no expiry should be treated as a standing grant and reviewed as such."]},{"heading":"A practical protocol","body":["Start with the identity provider export and the database grant list. Reconcile the two so every named engineer maps to a known role. Identify shared accounts and service credentials used by people, and replace them before measuring anything else.  The reconciliation should include service accounts that people use, because those are often the widest path. The reconciliation should be signed by the person who owns the production data.","Mark each role as read, export, or write for each data domain. Count the people in each role and the open exceptions. Pick the broadest role that is not required for daily support and scope it to the tasks the team actually handles. Set an expiry for the change and a review date.  The scoped role should be tested with a real support case before the old role is removed.","Check one removal end to end. Revoke a grant for a test account and confirm the engineer loses access to the console, the database, and any connected tool. A removal that works in one system but not another leaves a path the next review will miss.  The removal test should include the reporting tool and any saved query that caches credentials."]},{"heading":"A worked example","body":["A support pod of six engineers holds a read role on the production database. The role was granted for a single incident two years earlier and now covers every schema. The audit log shows that most lookups use three tables, while the rest of the role is unused.  The unused part of the role was the part nobody could justify when the review asked for a purpose.","The buyer replaces the role with a support view limited to those tables and a masked account column. Two engineers keep a temporary exception for billing investigations with a ninety-day expiry. The next review finds no shared credentials and a much smaller export path. The support work continues without a new approval for every case.  The exception should be reviewed at ninety days rather than renewed automatically."]},{"heading":"Data to collect first","body":["Record the account, the role, the data domains, the permission type, the grant date, the approver, and the expiry. Add whether the account is named or shared and whether export is allowed. Keep the identity provider export and the database grant list so the two can be compared.  The record should be stored where the identity team can see it, not only in the support pod's notes.","Collect a quarter of audit activity to see which grants are used. If the logs are incomplete, mark the gap rather than inferring use from tickets. An access review built on missing logs should say so and focus on removing the broadest grants first.  A missing log should be recorded as a gap with an owner, not filled with an assumption."]},{"heading":"Counterevidence and limits","body":["Broad access can be legitimate for a small team that handles urgent production support around the clock. Removing it without a replacement can slow a recovery or push engineers toward workarounds. The goal is a visible decision with a compensating control, not a uniform narrow grant for every team.  The compensating control should be named in the decision so the risk is visible to the next reviewer.","Access breadth also changes with the data model. A new table can fall inside an existing schema-wide role without any new approval. Review the role after a schema change as well as on a calendar. The measurement is a snapshot, and the underlying permissions can grow between reviews.  A schema change should trigger a breadth review even when no new account was created."]}],"sources":[{"name":"NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, Security and Privacy Controls","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final"},{"name":"NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/207/final"},{"name":"CISA Zero Trust Maturity Model","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/zero-trust-maturity-model"}],"keyStats":["3 permission types: read, export, and write","2 breadth dimensions: people with access and data each person can reach","4 fields per grant: scope, purpose, approver, and expiry","1 removal test: prove the access is gone in every connected system"],"takeaways":["Measure breadth by people and data domains, not by the number of accounts alone.","Treat export as a separate permission because a copy can leave the controlled system.","Replace shared credentials before measuring roles, or the count will understate real access.","Review exceptions first and give every one an expiry and an owner.","Prove one removal end to end so a partial revocation does not survive the next review."],"faqs":[{"question":"Is standing production access ever acceptable?","answer":"Yes, when the support need is continuous and the data is bounded. Make it a recorded decision with a review cadence and a compensating control such as query logging."},{"question":"What should a support console log?","answer":"Log the named user, the record or query, the purpose, and the time. The log should be reviewable without exposing the record itself to a wider audience."},{"question":"How often should breadth be reviewed?","answer":"At least quarterly, and after a schema change or a team composition change. A new table can widen an existing role without a new grant."}],"related":[{"title":"Write a production data access request for an offshore engineer","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-write-a-production-data-access-request-for-an-offshore-engineer"},{"title":"Run a quarterly access review for an offshore pod","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-run-a-quarterly-access-review-for-an-offshore-pod"},{"title":"Access provisioning delay in offshore developer onboarding","href":"/research/developeroffshore-access-provisioning-delay-in-offshore-developer-onboarding"}]},{"slug":"developeroffshore-legacy-service-takeover-risk-in-offshore-application-maintenance","title":"Legacy service takeover risk in offshore application maintenance","excerpt":"A research review of legacy service takeover risk: what the risk includes, hidden dependency inventory, runbook and rollback tests, access transfer, readiness measures, and a protocol.","published":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","sections":[{"heading":"Question and scope","body":["This report examines the risk an offshore pod takes on when it inherits maintenance of a legacy service. The unit is one service and the first quarter of ownership after the handover. The scope covers application code, scheduled jobs, data stores, deployment paths, and the operational knowledge needed to support them. It excludes new feature work that begins after the takeover.","The question is whether a buyer can distinguish a service the pod can actually operate from one that only looks transferred on an org chart. A signed handover document is not evidence that the team can deploy, roll back, or resolve a support case. The report treats takeover as a set of demonstrated capabilities rather than a date.  A capability that has not been tested is a claim, and the review should treat it as unverified."]},{"heading":"What takeover risk includes","body":["Takeover risk includes missing knowledge, missing access, and missing control. Missing knowledge shows up when the team cannot explain why a job runs at a particular hour. Missing access shows up when the deploy pipeline requires a credential the pod does not hold. Missing control shows up when only the former owner can approve a production change.  The three gaps often share a cause, such as a handover that transferred documents but not authority.","The risks interact. A team with access but no runbook will experiment on production. A team with a runbook but no access will wait for the former owner at every step. A takeover plan needs to close all three gaps before the former owner leaves, or the service is still dependent on them.  A plan that closes one gap while leaving the others open can make the service look safer than it is."]},{"heading":"Hidden dependency inventory","body":["Start with the repository and the deployment configuration, then look outside them. Scheduled jobs, cron entries, local scripts, and vendor dashboards often live in a different system from the application code. Each one is a dependency that the new team must find before an incident finds it.  The interview should ask what breaks when the outgoing engineer is on leave, because that answer names the real dependencies.","Ask the outgoing engineer to walk through a normal week rather than the architecture. The weekly tasks reveal the reports that are run by hand, the certificates that are renewed manually, and the customer who calls a specific person. Those details rarely appear in a design document and are exactly what a takeover loses.  The weekly walk-through should end with a list of tasks the pod has not yet performed."]},{"heading":"Runbook and rollback tests","body":["A runbook should be tested by the person who will use it, not by the person who wrote it. Give the incoming engineer a recent support case and ask them to follow the document without prompting. Record every question they need to ask and update the runbook with the answer.  The runbook test should use a case the incoming engineer has not seen, so the document carries the context.","Test the rollback in a non-production environment with the new team at the controls. Confirm the previous artifact is available, the data change is reversible, and the access works. A rollback that depends on the former owner's laptop is not a takeover control. Record the test date and the person who observed it.  The rollback test should include a failure that requires the previous artifact, not only a successful deploy."]},{"heading":"Access and release control","body":["List the accounts, tokens, and approvals the service needs for a normal release. Replace personal credentials with named accounts or a managed secret store before the handover closes. Confirm that the pod can start a deploy, approve its own routine change, and reach the logs without borrowing an administrator login.  The access list should name the person who can rotate each credential and the date of the last rotation.","Keep the buyer's approval for changes that cross an agreed risk boundary. The point of the transfer is to move routine operation, not to remove oversight. Write the boundary down so the pod knows which changes need a buyer signature and which do not.  The boundary should be written in the change template so a routine change does not wait for a signature it does not need."]},{"heading":"Measuring takeover readiness","body":["Use a small set of demonstrated capabilities rather than a completion percentage. The pod should be able to deploy the service without the former owner, roll it back, answer a support case from the runbook, and rotate a credential. Each capability is recorded as a yes or no with evidence and a date. A missing capability is a gap with an owner, not a lower score.","Track the open gaps with an owner and a target date. A gap that stays open for a quarter is a dependency that has been accepted, whether or not anyone said so. Review the list with the buyer at thirty and sixty days. The takeover is ready to close when the routine capabilities are demonstrated, even if documentation improvements continue."]},{"heading":"A practical protocol","body":["Build the inventory from four sources: the repository, the deployment configuration, the scheduled jobs, and an interview with the outgoing engineer. Mark each item as owned, shared, or unknown. Assign an owner to every unknown before the handover date.  An unknown item should have a named owner even when the owner is the person who will investigate it. The owner should also record where the inventory lives and who can edit it.","Run the deploy, rollback, and support-case tests with the incoming team. Record the result, the gaps, and the open questions. Replace personal credentials and confirm the pod can reach logs and monitoring. Put the remaining gaps into a plan with dates and named owners. The result should name the environment and the person who observed the test and the date it ran.","Review the plan at thirty and sixty days. Close the takeover only when the routine capabilities have been demonstrated. Keep the gap list visible after the close so the next owner inherits a service with a known history rather than a blank page.  The close review should confirm that the gap list is still accurate after the first month of ownership."]},{"heading":"A worked example","body":["A pod inherits a billing service with a clean repository and a short README. The first deploy fails because the pipeline needs a signing certificate stored on the former lead's machine. A scheduled invoice job also runs from a server that is not in the inventory, and its failure produces no alert.  The missing certificate was a control held by a person, which is the most fragile kind of access.","The team moves the certificate into the secret manager, adds the job to the deployment configuration, and creates an alert for a missed run. The rollback test exposes a manual database step that was never documented, so the team writes it down and rehearses it. The takeover closes after the pod completes a routine release and a support case without the former owner.  The team should add a check for the invoice job so a future failure produces an alert rather than a customer report."]},{"heading":"Data to collect first","body":["Record the service, the environments, the deploy path, the rollback path, the scheduled jobs, the external dependencies, and the named owners. Add the access method for each system and whether it is personal or shared. Keep the inventory in a place the pod can edit after the handover.  The inventory should be reviewed after every significant change, because a new job can appear without a new repository.","Record the capability tests with a date, a result, and the observer. A checklist with no evidence is a plan, not a takeover record. If an item cannot be tested, mark it as unverified and give it an owner rather than treating it as complete.  The observer should be someone who can confirm the capability, not only someone who attended the test."]},{"heading":"Counterevidence and limits","body":["Some legacy services are not worth a full takeover. A service scheduled for retirement may need only a support arrangement until the replacement ships. Spending a quarter on documentation for a system with six months left can be a poor use of the pod's time. Record that decision explicitly.  The retirement decision should be recorded with the end date and the support arrangement that replaces the takeover.","A successful takeover also depends on the outgoing team's cooperation, which the buyer may not control. If the former owner is unavailable, the pod must learn through testing and incident review. In that case, slow down the ownership transfer and keep a buyer-side escalation path until the capabilities are proven.  The buyer-side escalation path should stay in place until the pod has passed the capability tests."]}],"sources":[{"name":"NIST SP 800-128, Guide for Security-Focused Configuration Management","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/128/final"},{"name":"CISA Secure by Design","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/secure-by-design"},{"name":"Google SRE Book, Postmortem Culture","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/"}],"keyStats":["4 capability tests: deploy, rollback, support case, and credential rotation","3 risk gaps: knowledge, access, and control","4 inventory sources: repository, deploy configuration, scheduled jobs, and interview","1 close rule: the pod completes the routine work without the former owner"],"takeaways":["Treat takeover as demonstrated capability, not a signed date or a completed document.","Inventory scheduled jobs and manual tasks outside the repository, because they are easy to miss.","Test the runbook with the incoming engineer on a real case and record the questions.","Replace personal credentials before closing the handover so the pod can operate independently.","Review the gap list at thirty and sixty days and close only when routine work is proven."],"faqs":[{"question":"What is the first takeover test?","answer":"A deploy from the normal pipeline with the incoming team at the controls. It exposes the artifact source, the approval path, and any personal credential the pipeline depends on."},{"question":"Should the former owner stay available?","answer":"A short overlap is useful for questions, but the takeover should not depend on it. Name a cutoff date and test the capabilities before it arrives."},{"question":"How do we handle an undocumented manual task?","answer":"Write the steps down, run them with the new owner, and add a check that fails when the task is missed. A task that only lives in memory is an unowned dependency."}],"related":[{"title":"Run a takeover audit when an offshore pod inherits a service","href":"/blog/developeroffshore-run-a-takeover-audit-when-an-offshore-pod-inherits-a-service"},{"title":"Documentation ownership when an offshore pod maintains a legacy system","href":"/research/developeroffshore-documentation-ownership-when-an-offshore-pod-maintains-a-legacy-system"},{"title":"Knowledge-transfer risk when a developer leaves a distributed team","href":"/research/developeroffshore-knowledge-transfer-risk-when-a-developer-leaves-a-distributed-team"}]}]
